course: dictionary
lesson: 6106
title: Miasma and the bad-air idea
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: A miasma is a cloud of bad-smelling, unhealthy air, like the smell from rotting things or a swamp. «Before germ theory, many people believed illness came from bad air, called miasma.» The words bad air and illness are the clues: miasma is the bad air people blamed for sickness.
explain: Miasma can also mean a heavy, unpleasant feeling that hangs over a place, as in «a miasma of gloom». Gloom means sadness or darkness, so this miasma is a thick, sad feeling spread around. Check it by swapping in a meaning: «a cloud of gloom» still makes sense, so you have found it.
explain: In history, miasma theory was the old idea that diseases were caused by bad air. People believed this until the 1800s. Then scientists found that germs spread illness, not smells, so the miasma idea was wrong. A word can belong to a true meaning and a past mistake at the same time.
fact: «Miasma» means a cloud of bad-smelling, unhealthy air.
fact: «Miasma theory» was the old belief that diseases were caused by bad air.
fact: Miasma theory was believed until the 1800s, before germ theory showed that germs spread disease.
fact: John Snow's work on cholera in London in 1854 helped show that diseases are spread by germs, not smells.
fact: «A miasma of gloom» means a heavy, unhappy feeling that hangs over a place.
exercise: «Before germ theory, many people believed illness came from bad air, called miasma.» What does miasma mean? | *A cloud of bad-smelling, unhealthy air | A sweet perfume from flowers | A bright light in the sky | A loud noise from a factory
exercise: «A miasma of gloom hung over the old mill.» What does miasma suggest here? | A clean, fresh breeze | *A heavy, unpleasant feeling over a place | A happy party with music | A strong wind from the sea
exercise: Ella reads that miasma theory was believed until the 1800s. What was miasma theory? | The modern idea that germs spread disease | *The old idea that diseases were caused by bad air | A theory about how stars move | A rule for cooking food safely
test: «The swamp gave off a miasma that made the children cough.» What does miasma mean in this sentence? | *Bad-smelling, unhealthy air from the swamp | A loud splash in the water | A bright patch of sunshine | A clean stream of water
test: Why did scientists like John Snow later reject miasma theory? | Because the air in London was always clean | Because cholera was not a disease | *Because they found germs spread diseases, not smells | Because nobody lived in London in 1854
exam: Which sentence uses miasma to mean a heavy, unpleasant feeling? | The rain fell softly on the roof | The children played happily in the park | The bread smelled warm and sweet | *A miasma of sadness hung over the empty town
